r the removal and insertion of the smaller cubes.\n\n1. **Surface Area of the Original Cube:**\n\n   The side length of the original cube is 3 inches. The surface area \\(A\\) of a cube with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   A = 6s^2\n   \\]\n   For \\(s = 3\\):\n   \\[\n   A = 6 \\times 3^2 = 6 \\times 9 = 54 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Effect of Cutting Out 1-inch Cubes:**\n\n   Cutting out a 1-inch cube from each corner removes a small amount of surface area but also exposes new faces. Each corner cut-out exposes 3 new faces of 1 square inch each:\n   \\[\n   3 \\times 1 \\times 1 = 3 \\text{ square inches per corner}\n   \\]\n   Since there are 8 corners:\n   \\[\n   8 \\times 3 = 24 \\text{ square inches of new surface area}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Effect of Inserting 2-inch Cubes:**\n\n   Inserting a 2-inch cube into each corner adds more surface area. Each 2-inch cube has a surface area of:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 2^2 = 6 \\times 4 = 24 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n   However, since each 2-inch cube is inserted into the corner, only the faces not touching the original cube are exposed. Each 2-inch cube inserted into a corner exposes 3 new faces of 4 square inches each:\n   \\[\n   3 \\times 4 = 12 \\text{ square inches per corner}\n   \\]\n   Since there are 8 corners:\n   \\[\n   8 \\times 12 = 96 \\text{ square inches of new surface area}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Total Surface Area Calculation:**\n\n   The total surface area of the resulting solid is the sum of the original surface area, the new surface area from cutting out the 1-inch cubes, and the new surface area from inserting the 2-inch cubes:\n   \\[\n   54 + 24 + 96 = 174 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of square inches in the surface area of the resulting solid is \\(\\boxed{174}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{174}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
